    "q-28331": {
      "dom_id": "step-q28331",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)(x+2)(x+3)?$ \n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that the function is a product of $3$ linear binomials, so this function is a cubic function. Moreover, it is a positive cubic because if we were to expand the parentheses, the coefficient of the ${x}^{3}$ term would be positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)(x+2)(x+3)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x+⋯=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+⋯\n$$\n\nThe equation is already in factored form. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the roots are $x=1,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=-3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}(0-1)(0+2)(0+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)(2)(3)}{2} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-3,-2,1$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-3,$ as follows:\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234519/assets/8bc52ee56cc821a2d498ebee872c620a61c527f0967b3cb6de985bd10ffce97a.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
