    "q-112010": {
      "dom_id": "step-q112010",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(x+4)(x+2)(2-x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that the function is a product of $3$ linear binomials, so this function is a cubic function. Moreover, it is a negative cubic because if we were to expand the parentheses, the coefficient of the ${x}^{3}$ term would be negative:\n\n$$\n(x+4)(x+2)(2-x)=x\\cdot x\\cdot (-x)+⋯=-{x}^{3}+⋯\n$$\n\nThe equation is given in the factored form. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the roots are  $x=-4,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=2.$ \n\nWe now find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0+4)(0+2)(2-0) \\\\  & =(4)(2)(2) \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-4,-2,2$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=16,$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234519/assets/06e69e393a48794262d45233bdd0df8f69f2b9ab8a3e9acee5aed3280db9f503.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
