      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Given that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+7x+6,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "First, we notice that the function is a negative cubic curve because the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is negative.\n\nWe are told that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so to find the other roots of $f(x)$ we use synthetic division to divide $f(x)$ by $(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 7 & 6 \\\\  &  & 1 & -1 & -6 \\\\  & -1 & 1 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+1)(-{x}^{2}+x+6) \\\\  & =-(x+1)({x}^{2}-x-6) \\\\  & =-(x+1)(x+2)(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to zero, we find that the roots of $f(x)$ are $x=-1,x=-2,$ and $x=3.$\n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0+1)(0+2)(0-3) \\\\  & =-(1)(2)(-3) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an $S$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-2,-1,3$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=6,$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14234519/assets/d8bd6f9c8cbfd1872871a00d403e3e8980c081b03caac5f2c5cc656161a00cd5.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
